440: ambiguity.  Together with simple connectivity this proves that
441: the total manifold is a homotopy six-sphere, and \(\Theta_6=0\)
442: identifies its smooth oriented type with the standard sphere.
443: 
444: **Proof: the closed positive parameter loop.** Write \(t_c\) for
445: the chosen nodal zero and put
446: \(h(t)=(t-t_c)/t\).  Since
447: \(\gamma(z,v+1)=\gamma(z,v)+\delta\), the smooth logarithmic
448: gluing changes by \((\log t)/(2\pi i)\delta\) under the
449: positive parameter deck.  The global ordinary deck is
450: \(-\log h(t)\delta/(2\pi i)\): near \(t=0\) it has exactly
451: that value up to a holomorphic unit logarithm, which extends on
452: the cap. More explicitly, write
453: \[
454:  D_{\rm ord}=-\frac{\log h(t)}{2\pi i}\delta,\qquad
455:  D_{\rm cap}=-\frac{\log(t-t_c)}{2\pi i}\delta.
456: \]
457: The second logarithm is holomorphic on the chosen smooth cap.
458: The directed gluing satisfies the exact compatibility
459: \[
460:  D_{\rm ord}\circ\Psi_v=\Psi_{v+1}\circ D_{\rm cap},
461:  \qquad
462:  D_{\rm ord}=D_{\rm cap}+\frac{\log t}{2\pi i}\delta .
463: \]
464: Here the \(D\)'s denote the respective translations from \(v\)
465: to \(v+1\). At the cusp use \(q=h(t)\), \(s=\log q/(2\pi i)\).
466: Then the actual periods are
467: 
468: \[
469:  \gamma(s,v)=\binom{\Gamma(q)}{C(q)+s+v},\qquad
470:  \delta=\binom01,
471: \]
472: 
473: with \(\Gamma,C\) holomorphic at zero, and the deck translates
474: by \(-s\delta\).  Its line-character multiplier \(q^{-1}\)
475: extends through the cusp: compose an inverse gamma lattice shift
476: with the bounded source translation \(\Gamma\) and the
477: nonvanishing multiplier \(\lambda e^{2\pi iC}\).  At every
478: other original fibre \(h\) is a unit.  This proves that the
479: glued family descends holomorphically over the punctured
480: \(\lambda\)-disc, with the stated positive closing map.
481: 
482: Here \(\gamma\) is the inward period of the actual contracting
483: suspension. In the physical Abel and line coordinates it is
484: \[
485:  B(a,V)=
486:  \bigl(a+\Gamma(q),\,e^{2\pi iC(q)}q\lambda V\bigr),
487:  \qquad \Gamma(q)=\log_{\rm Tate}(-G).
488: \]
489: For small \(q,\lambda\), its positive real direction decreases
490: \(\log|V|\). This fixes its orientation relative to the source
491: page. With \(\delta\) the positive phase of \(V\), the complex
492: orientation gives
493: \[
494:  \operatorname{or}(Y)
495:    =-\operatorname{or}(S)\,d\log|V|
496:    =\operatorname{or}(S)\,d\gamma .
497: \]
498: Thus the positive gamma in the Hopf page below is this inward
499: period. The normalized smooth norm coordinate increases in
500: this direction.
501: 
502: For a completely marked angular comparison use the auxiliary
503: logarithmic cover \(q=Q_0^2\), and the two origins
504: 
505: \[
506:  o=\binom{-\Gamma s}{-Cs-s^2/2-sv},\qquad
507:  \sigma=\binom{\Gamma v}{Cv+(v^2-v)/2}.
508: \]
509: 
510: The first has parameter difference \(-s\delta\); the second
511: has difference \(-s\delta+\gamma\).  Under \(s\mapsto s+2\),
512: their differences are \(-2\gamma-2\delta\) and zero.  Thus
513: they descend on this auxiliary cover.  In contrast, the ordinary
514: origin has one-step difference \(-\gamma-\delta/2\); we do
515: not impose a false one-step periodicity.  The cusp origin extends
516: by its bounded unit characters.  Subtracting the ordinary origin
517: removes the parameter deck and gives the one-node parameter chain
518: \(X_i=V/\lambda^i\), \(Y_i=\lambda^{i+1}/V\),
519: \(X_iY_i=\lambda\), over every nonzero \(q\).  Its origin
520: extends.  These are the two extending collar origins.  No proper
521: two-variable corner or descent of such a corner is used.
522: 
523: Their exact difference is
524: 
525: \[
526:  F=\binom{\Gamma(s+v)}{C(s+v)+(s+v)^2/2-v/2},
527: \]
528: 
529: and hence
530: 
531: \[
532:  F(s,v+\theta)-F(s,v)
533:  =\theta\gamma(s,v)+\frac{\theta(\theta-1)}2\delta.
534: \]
535: 
536: The additional line gauge is periodic, equals one at both
537: endpoints, and vanishes in the circle quotient.  The double cusp
538: deck changes this angular difference by \(2\theta\delta\),
539: so the retained line gauge has winding two.  In particular the
540: actual positive \(\lambda\)-loop makes one positive gamma turn,
541: with no parameter cover.  Every identity is executed in
542: `code/part3_fourfold/os45_origins.py`.
543: 
544: More explicitly, the ordinary and cusp coordinates are
545: \(\zeta-o\) and \(\zeta-\sigma\), so the cusp-to-ordinary
546: transition is translation by \(F=\sigma-o\). Its angular
547: increment is the positive translation displayed above. This
548: identifies the moving fixed sphere in the constant orbit with
549: the positive inward gamma motion used by the real trace.
550: 
